{"book":{"id":"book-the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-underworld-and-the-american-dream-patrick-radden-kee","slug":"the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-underworld-and-the-american-dream-patrick-radden-kee","title":"The Snakehead: An Epic Tale of the Chinatown Underworld and the American Dream","authors":[{"id":"person-patrick-radden-keefe","name":"Patrick Radden Keefe"}],"publisherId":"publisher-wydawnictwo-czarne","isbn13":["9780385521307"],"subjects":["True Crime & Justice"],"topics":["Immigration, Refugees & Borderlands","Business, Capitalism & Corporations","Crime, Policing & Violence","Housing, Cities & Urban Life"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=The%20Snakehead%3A%20An%20Epic%20Tale%20of%20the%20Chinatown%20Underworld%20and%20the%20American%20Dream%20Patrick%20Radden%20Keefe","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=The%20Snakehead%3A%20An%20Epic%20Tale%20of%20the%20Chinatown%20Underworld%20and%20the%20American%20Dream%20Patrick%20Radden%20Keefe","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=The%20Snakehead%3A%20An%20Epic%20Tale%20of%20the%20Chinatown%20Underworld%20and%20the%20American%20Dream%20Patrick%20Radden%20Keefe","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=The%20Snakehead%3A%20An%20Epic%20Tale%20of%20the%20Chinatown%20Underworld%20and%20the%20American%20Dream%20Patrick%20Radden%20Keefe","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL13655645W"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-kapuscinski-reportage-wikipedia-nagroda-im-ryszarda-kapuscinskiego-za-reportaz-literacki","source-open-library-the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-underworld-and-the-american-dream-patrick-radden-kee","source-open-library-summary-the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-underworld-and-the-american-dream-patrick-radden-kee"],"publicationYear":2009,"thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/6302340-L.jpg","renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":48,"criticalRenown":62,"controversy":18,"llmAffinity":90,"affinityResidual":11.03,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.97,"criticalRenown":0.95,"controversy":0.88,"llmAffinity":0.98},"tags":{"craft":"narrative-drive","evidence":"reportage","stance":"moral-seriousness"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"A mesmerizing narrative about the rise and fall of an unlikely international crime bossIn the 1980s, a wave of Chinese from Fujian province began arriving in America. Like other immigrant groups before them, they showed up with little money but with an intense work ethic and an unshakeable belief in the promise of the United States. Many of them lived in a world outside the law, working in a shadow economy overseen by the ruthless gangs that ruled the narrow streets of New York's Chinatown.The figure who came to dominate this Chinese underworld was a middle-aged grandmother known as Sister Ping. Her path to the American dream began with an unusual business run out of a tiny noodle store on Hester Street. From her perch above the shop, Sister Ping ran a full-service underground bank for illegal Chinese immigrants. But her real business-a business that earned an estimated $40 million-was smuggling people. As a \"snakehead,\" she built a complex--and often vicious--global conglomerate, relying heavily on familial ties, and employing one of Chinatown's most violent gangs to protect her power and profits. Like an underworld CEO, Sister Ping created an intricate smuggling network that stretched from Fujian Province to Hong Kong to Burma to Thailand to Kenya to Guatemala to Mexico.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Illegal aliens","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-underworld-and-the-american-dream-patrick-radden-kee"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Emigration and immigration","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-snakehead-an-epic-tale-of-the-chinatown-un